/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Behind the dashboard sits a microcontroller with memory, a CAN transceiver and display drivers.
   - The fuel sender is a float on an arm that moves a wiper over a resistor track in the tank, so the resistance changes with the fuel level.
   - Telltales use fixed colours.
   - The speedometer does not use engine rpm.
   - v is the displayed speed in km/h, n the whole number of pulses counted in a gate time of T seconds, d the distance per pulse in metres, and 3.6 converts m/s to km/h.
   - Counting whole pulses means one pulse is the smallest step: 3.6 × d ÷ T km/h.
   - A modern needle is driven by a small stepper motor through a reduction gear.
   - A segmented LCD shows fixed digits cheaply; a colour TFT shows menus, a camera image or a map.
   - A head-up display (HUD) projects from a unit in the dashboard onto a small combiner glass or onto a wedge-shaped layer in the windscreen.
*/
