/* book Worked example: Q: A wheel speed sensor gives N = 4 pulses per wheel revolution and the tyre rolling circumference is C = 1.90 m. The cluster counts whole pulses in a gate of T = 0.5 s. At a true 100 km/h find (a) the distance per pulse, (b) the pulse frequency, (c) the pulses counted in one gate, (d) the displayed speed and (e) the speed step of one pulse.
   1. d = C ÷ N = 1.90 ÷ 4 = 0.475 m per pulse (K = 1000 ÷ 0.475 = 2105 pulses/km).
   2. 100 km/h = 100 ÷ 3.6 = 27.78 m/s; wheel speed = 27.78 ÷ 1.90 = 14.62 rev/s; f = 14.62 × 4 = 58.48 pulses/s.
   3. Pulses in 0.5 s = 58.48 × 0.5 = 29.24; the counter keeps whole pulses, so n = 29.
   4. v = 3.6 × 29 × 0.475 ÷ 0.5 = 99.18 km/h.
   5. One pulse is worth 3.6 × 0.475 ÷ 0.5 = 3.42 km/h, so a single gate can only show 95.8, 99.2, 102.6 … km/h.
   Answer: (a) 0.475 m; (b) 58.48 pulses/s; (c) 29; (d) 99.2 km/h, 0.8 km/h low because the part pulse is dropped; (e) 3.42 km/h per pulse, which is why clusters average several gates. */
