/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A windscreen wiper motor is a permanent-magnet DC motor: two ferrite magnets give the field, and current reaches the armature through carbon brushes on a commutator.
   - Most wiper motors have three brushes.
   - The park switch is a cam-driven contact in the gearbox, not the stalk switch.
   - The intermittent relay runs the motor for one wipe, waits, then repeats.
   - T is the delay in seconds, R the timing resistance in ohms and C the capacitance in farads.
   - The washer system has a plastic reservoir, a small permanent-magnet motor driving a centrifugal pump that draws a few amperes, tubes with a non-return valve so the jets prime at once, and jets or fan nozzles aimed at the screen.
   - An electric horn is a vibrating-diaphragm buzzer.
   - Remote keyless entry (RKE) is a one-way radio link.
   - Passive keyless entry removes the button: aerials in the door handles send a low-frequency wake-up at about 125 kHz, the fob answers on UHF, and the door unlocks when the handle is touched; with the fob inside, the start button works.
   - The immobiliser is a separate check.
*/
